DISK MANAGEMENT COMMANDS
**********************************************************
1. Check the disk usage :

Shows the filesystem usage and avaliable spaces

 Command : df -h

 command : df -i --> display the inode usage instead of storage size for all mounted files

 Example : df -i /var 

 df --> displays the filesystem usage
 -i --> displays the inode usage instaed of storage size
 /var --> check the file system containing


2. Check the Directory Size

 Shows the total Directory size

 Command : du -sh

 Example : du -sh /var/log


3.View BLock Devices

 List all disk and paritions

  Command : lsblk

  Command : lsblk -f 

  -f is shows the additional shows the filesystem information 

4. View Physical Volume

  Command : pvs

  pvs shows the physical storage supplied to LVM.

  PFree = 0 : It means all physical volume [Eg:19GB] has been allocated to logical volumes
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Physical Volume :

5. Create a physical Volume

  Command : pvcreate /dev/nvme0n2p1

  verify : pvs
   
  Command : pvdisplay 

  the above command display the detailed information about the pv information

  Example : pvdisplay /dev/nvem0n2p1
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Volume Groups

6. View the Volume Groups

  Command : vgs

  vgs shows the storage pool formed from physical volumes

  it displays :

  -> Volume Group Name 
  ->Number of Pysical Volumes
  ->Number of Logical Volumes
  ->Total Voulme Group Size
  ->Free space available for creating or extending Logical Volumes

  attr = wz--n : It means Volume Groups is writable and active

  VFree = 0 : It means No free space remains in the Volume Group

  Command : vgcreate 

  used to create volume group

  Example : vgcreate vg_data /dev/nevme0n2p1

  vg_data --> the name assigned to it


  Command : vgdisplay 

  used to display the detailed information about the volume Group

  Example : vgdisplay vg_data /dev/nevme0n2p1
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Logical Volume :


 6. Show Logical Volumes
 
  Command : lvs

  lvs shows the usable logical disks created from the volume group.

  it shows the logical volumes , volume groups and Size

  if Attr = -wi-ao---- : 

  w — Logical volume is writable.
  i — Uses normal linear allocation.
  a — Logical volume is active.
  o — Logical volume is open and currently in use. 

  Command : lvcreate 

  Used to Create a Logical Volume(lvs)

  Example : lvcreate -L 1G -n lv_data vg_data

  -n : used to assign name to logical volume 
  vg_data : selects the our Volume Group

  Verify : lvs

 Command : lvdisplay 

 show the detailed information 

 Example : lvdisplay /dev/vg_data/lv_data

 Command : lvextend 

 Example : lvextend -r -L +500 /dev/vg_data/lv_data

